#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Polynomials is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "105104fe-d88f-5dd8-9b3a-02a1ef01ef38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555032} {:answer/id #uuid "20c9cb4a-c332-5bc5-8dd5-fed7de433c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555062} {:answer/id #uuid "63ad22ea-66fa-50fc-bde7-f0f232d88853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555224} {:answer/id #uuid "68ff13dc-9101-5cf2-bc69-4e700b97b26f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555239} {:answer/id #uuid "b938e797-07a9-546d-9bec-948b3e7a15fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "105104fe-d88f-5dd8-9b3a-02a1ef01ef38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555032} :answer-field/id #uuid "980312ed-2719-5060-9fbb-108e85ad677e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4f61444-c8ba-5ff9-98c2-7150307d9838" :question/math-academy-id "q-100121" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-1,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2t+2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2-2t.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-1,2t+2⟩ \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{-1}\\cdot (2t+2){|}_{-1}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (2t+2){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)-1)\\cdot (2(-1)+2)+(1-1)\\cdot (2(1)+2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t+2,2-2t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t+2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{-1}+(2t+2){|}_{1}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(2(-1)+2)\\cdot (2-2(-1))+(2(1)+2)\\cdot (2-2(1)) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-1,2-2t⟩ \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{-1}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{-1}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)-1)\\cdot (2-2(-1))+(1-1)\\cdot (2-2(1)) \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39692b40-7d68-5ae5-9fe1-0f8b0b2e0d77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c5caa67-c911-5ad0-8f2b-aa4c00b90cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{t}^{3}+3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238292} {:answer/id #uuid "32e18caa-4580-5ff6-9760-b3dcb3dac655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238498} {:answer/id #uuid "8e55c40f-86f3-5748-8fdb-5fa0410ee712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239290} {:answer/id #uuid "e76649ac-05ad-5f2e-a3c3-ed56c8b3436f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240092} {:answer/id #uuid "f85b144c-8163-5666-ae5c-309d42c47eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+6t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32e18caa-4580-5ff6-9760-b3dcb3dac655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238498} :answer-field/id #uuid "f02b5045-4774-5001-9f14-da28bf31cb04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2794de9d-8dc5-594e-8a2b-e907f92bf64b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105100" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$ \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=3t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}+1,\\,g(t)=10{t}^{3}\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)\\}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{1}(t)\\}$ equals $6t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=6t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{2}(t)\\}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{3}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $10{t}^{3}$ is an odd function, while ${t}^{2}+1$ is even. Hence, their product $10{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-1,1],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{0}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)=0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =6t+0 \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119837 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf29808-4551-56a0-ac86-a4587a06fc0f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Point of a Line and a Plane Given in Other Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "613bfe5f-02e2-5735-9f79-164b162ce348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-1,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442964} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b4a532-3b78-5ed1-a6e7-746b7ec7ab3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,-3,16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442969} {:answer/id #uuid "d748dd3f-d487-5117-bef8-07f995fa3631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442974} {:answer/id #uuid "de970a68-1f18-57df-a82c-3fb7858ebc0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,5,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442975} {:answer/id #uuid "f85e9e92-164c-5dbe-a1e2-300ee4897290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f85e9e92-164c-5dbe-a1e2-300ee4897290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442977} :answer-field/id #uuid "817f92e5-c306-5bc0-98bb-5216ce23f4dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2968eff5-6f3b-5cf4-97d0-686663582eb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-108369" :question/problem "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:r=⟨-2,1,4⟩+t⟨3,2,-6⟩,\\,Π:-4x+3y-z-11=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the equation of the line as\n\n$$\nr=⟨-2+3t,1+2t,4-6t⟩.\n$$\n\nLet's also write the equation of the plane in the dot product form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+3y-z-11 & =0 \\\\ -4x+3y-z & =11 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,3,-1⟩ & =11 \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,3,-1⟩ & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨-2+3t,1+2t,4-6t⟩$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨-4,3,-1⟩ & =11 \\\\ ⟨-2+3t,1+2t,4-6t⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,3,-1⟩ & =11 \\\\ -4(-2+3t)+3(1+2t)-(4-6t) & =11 \\\\ 8-12t+3+6t-4+6t & =11 \\\\ 7 & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has no solution. Therefore, we conclude that the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ do not intersect."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329741 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca46ab2-488a-5596-b96b-559463731556" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors Under a Given Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65117d9d-ac17-5c3c-ad23-40504ec9738e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327814} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe2c1bf-0d41-5bd6-852b-bcb47dca1f2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327906} {:answer/id #uuid "a36a262a-8e9c-5530-80c7-c4dcef50c604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328346} {:answer/id #uuid "c69e47ae-8ddc-53a7-bcd5-6cfe3241a999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328681} {:answer/id #uuid "cad333f1-fc80-560c-a3ad-32d711fb17e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65117d9d-ac17-5c3c-ad23-40504ec9738e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327814} :answer-field/id #uuid "f23c2ed0-a69d-5485-9217-a853a3ba817c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36e3ad83-f361-5d68-9744-a8a366bc0803" :question/math-academy-id "q-43836" :question/problem "The linear transformation $T$ has the standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat are the respective images of the standard basis vectors ${i,j}$ of ${R}^{2}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nThe images of the vectors in the standard basis ${i,j}$ are the first and second columns of $T,$ respectively. \n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(j) & =T\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the images of the vectors in the standard basis ${i,j}$ by computing the matrix products $T\\cdot i$ and $T\\cdot j:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T\\cdot i=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(j) & =T\\cdot j=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162014 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84207049-12f7-514c-bfa2-51bce8631b41" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Projection Matrix That Orthogonally Projects a Vector to the Subspace Spanned by an Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2ad9ead-87b5-5eaf-8e9e-875db0473a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395047} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc2aec0-03c1-52f2-822c-b5b8cc4452d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395048} {:answer/id #uuid "a3017386-cdc1-5c67-aaa3-da1bf9ee139b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395049} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae42751-57d2-54a9-a3c9-6c4df9c9f145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395050} {:answer/id #uuid "f48f3f42-28e8-5a6e-8c4e-682350ff961b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3017386-cdc1-5c67-aaa3-da1bf9ee139b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395049} :answer-field/id #uuid "659dcf86-3da2-503b-9a49-309b10754236" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a7d41eb-9b50-57b5-bc80-ebc06a06e4f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95708" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+1I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations which is already in row echelon form and has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=1.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
